This is an execution runbook for asset integration once the C++ codebase is compiled. 

STEP 0 — Before you start
- Make sure you have Unreal Engine 5 open and your project loaded.
- Put your meshes, textures, animations, VFX, and SFX into one folder in the project, e.g. /Game/Import/.
- Keep a small test set of 1–3 weapons to try first.

STEP 1 — Get the AI mapping package
- Ask the AI to give you the UE5 mapping package (it will include Python scripts, a manifest template, DataAsset and Blueprint templates, tests, and a runbook).
- Save the package into your project folder.

STEP 2 — Fill the import manifest
- Open import_manifest.json (a simple text file).
- For each weapon, add:
  - id: a unique name (like sword_iron_01)
  - display_name: "Iron Sword"
  - mesh_path: "/Game/Import/Weapons/sword_iron.fbx"
  - textures: list of texture paths
  - sockets: ["GripSocket","VFX_Spawn"]
  - montages: ["AM_Sword_Light","AM_Sword_Heavy"]
  - vfx: ["VFX_Sword_Slash"]
  - base_stats: { base_damage: 12.5, attack_speed: 1.0 }
- Save the file.

STEP 3 — Run the importer script (inside Unreal)
- Open the Python console in Unreal (Window → Developer Tools → Python Console).
- Run:
  python path/to/importer.py --manifest "Content/Import/import_manifest.json"
- The script will:
  - Import FBX and textures
  - Create materials and material instances
  - Add sockets to meshes
  - Create hitbox collision components
  - Make DA_Weapon DataAssets and fill fields
  - Make BP_Weapon Blueprints and link the DataAssets
  - Assign montages and VFX references

STEP 4 — Run the quick tests (Play In Editor)
- In Unreal, run the provided Editor tests:
  - Use the test runner or run the script: RunEditorTests --tests=WeaponMappingTests
- Tests check:
  - No missing references
  - GripSocket exists
  - Hitbox roughly matches the mesh
  - Equip → Attack → Hit flow fires events
  - Deterministic RNG test (same seed → same result)

STEP 5 — Fix any failures (iteration)
- If a test fails:
  - Save the test log file (WeaponMappingTests_results.json).
  - Copy the failing asset entry from import_manifest.json.
  - Give both to the AI (paste logs + manifest snippet).
  - The AI will suggest a fix or update the importer script.
  - Re-run the importer for the failing asset and re-run tests.
- Repeat until tests pass for the sample set.

STEP 6 — Bulk import all assets
- When sample tests pass, update import_manifest.json with all assets.
- Run:
  python path/to/importer.py --manifest "Content/Import/import_manifest_full.json" --batch
- Watch logs and fix any new failures as in STEP 5.

STEP 7 — Integration checks
- Run integration tests that exercise:
  - Floating origin + streaming in a test map
  - Combat with multiple weapons and enemies
  - Network replication (if multiplayer)
- Fix issues the AI flags or that appear in logs.

STEP 8 — Profile and tune
- Use Unreal Profiler to check performance.
- Tune hitbox sizes, socket offsets, LODs, and streaming settings.

STEP 9 — Acceptance (what “done” looks like)
- No missing references in logs.
- Equip → Attack → Hit works for test weapons.
- VFX spawns at the right socket and time.
- Deterministic tests pass for seeded systems.
- Automated tests pass in PIE.

STEP 10 — If anything still fails
- Collect logs and screenshots.
- Paste them to the AI and ask: “Fix importer script for these failures.”
- Re-run the updated script and tests until green.

Quick tips:
- Start small (1–3 weapons) before bulk runs.
- Use consistent names for files and assets.
- Keep logs and manifest snippets handy for fast fixes.